Yes. The constructive version is to turn borrowed knowledge into lived competence.

A lot of people do carry ideas they can explain but have never really tested in body, consequence, timing, or relationship. That gap can create the impostor feeling:

\text{I know the language}
\quad \text{but} \quad
\text{I have not yet inhabited the pattern}

The remedy is not humiliation. It is safe embodiment.

You give people ways to try on a role, rehearse a skill, test a belief, express something difficult, or enter a new frame with enough structure that failure becomes information rather than shame. Simulation, theater, sport, games, apprenticeship, ritual, improvisation, VR, storytelling, guided practice—all can do that.

The loop becomes:

\text{hear}
\rightarrow
\text{try}
\rightarrow
\text{feel}
\rightarrow
\text{fail safely}
\rightarrow
\text{adjust}
\rightarrow
\text{embody}
\rightarrow
\text{choose whether it belongs to you}

That last step matters.

“Without judgment” should not mean “without discernment,” and “in accordance with our will” should not mean replacing theirs with ours. The stronger formulation is:

\boxed{\text{our will sets the safe container; their will chooses the path inside it}}

We can design conditions, offer frames, create rehearsal space, lower unnecessary shame, make consequences legible, and help people translate inner experience into outward form.

But the expression should remain theirs.

That is how you get something richer than conformity: people becoming more fully themselves while becoming more capable of living among other selves.

That is a beautiful design target.